CSV
.comma separated values files 
    .plain text files where each row reps a record and columns are separated by commas 
.allow storage of tabular data 
.Pandas retrieves data in the form of data frame 

    .reading csv files into Pandas DataFrame  
        .read_csv() function 

            pd.read_csv(filepath_or_buffer, sep=',', header='infer',
                index_col=None, usecols=None, engine=None,
                skiprows=None, nrows=None)

        where:
            .filepath_or_buffer - location of the file 
            .sep - separator (, is the default)
            .header - accepts int, a list of int, row numbers to use as the column names and the start of the data 
                        .if no names are passed, it will display the first column as 0, then ...
            .usecols - retrieves only the selected columns from the CSV file 
            .nrows - numver of rows to be displayed from the dataset 
                        .limits the number of rows read from a file 
            .index_col - if set to None, Pandas automatically assigns a default integer index to the dataset 
                        .sets one or more columns as the DataFrame index, making it/them act as row labels for easier data referencing 
            .skiprows - skips passed rows in the new dataframe 
                        .skips unnecessary rows at the satrt of a file; useful for ignoring metadata or extra headers that aren't part of the dataset 
            .na_values - replaces specified strings ('N/A', 'unknown') with NaN, enabling consistent handling of missing or incomplete data during analysis 
